  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Legal annual cap on migration that falls each year, higher salary thresholds, visa fee rises, dependants restrictions, monthly removals to Rwanda; new protest controls (face coverings bans, cumulative impact), tougher sentences, more prisons, expanded police powers; mandatory National Service; deepen EU ties without ceding sovereignty; single‑sex accommodation rights and Cass Review; defence spending to 2.5% of GDP. TEXT_G: Repeal anti‑protest measures, uphold HRA/ECHR, Digital Bill of Rights; protect reproductive rights, modernise gender recognition incl. non‑binary, ban conversion practices, regulated cannabis market; end Hostile Environment, scrap Rwanda plan, safe/legal routes, right to work after 3 months, reverse family visa threshold rises; rebuild EU relations with Single Market aim and Erasmus+ return; tackle disproportionate stop and search and prioritise diversion over punishment.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_F argues from TAN commitments—law‑and‑order, restrictive immigration, national service, and sovereignty—while TEXT_G argues from GAL commitments—expanded civil liberties, social liberalisation, and liberal immigration and supranationalism. Because the electorate profile is modestly TAN‑leaning (0.60 TAN, 0.40 GAL), TEXT_F aligns more closely with it.",
